<span id="id5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">carlesd@microsoft.com</span></span></span>
</span> and 
<span id="id6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Dylan J. Foster</span></span>
<span id="id6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Microsoft Research</span></span></span>
<span id="id6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id6.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">dylanfoster@microsoft.com</span></span></span>
</span> and 
<span id="id7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Akshay Krishnamurthy</span></span>
<span id="id7.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id7.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id7.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Microsoft Research</span></span></span>
<span id="id7.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id7.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id7.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">akshaykr@microsoft.com</span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Inference-time methods that aggregate and prune multiple samples have emerged as a powerful paradigm for steering large language models, yet we lack any principled understanding of their accuracy–cost tradeoffs.
In this paper, we introduce a route to rigorously study such approaches using the lens of <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">particle filtering</em> algorithms such as Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC).
Given a base language model and a <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">process reward model</em> estimating expected terminal rewards, we ask: <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how accurately can we sample from a target distribution given some number of process reward evaluations?</em>
Theoretically, we identify (1) simple criteria enabling non-asymptotic guarantees for SMC; (2) algorithmic improvements to SMC; and (3) a fundamental limit faced by all particle filtering methods. Empirically, we demonstrate that our theoretical criteria effectively govern the <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sampling error</em> of SMC, though not necessarily its final <em id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">accuracy</em>, suggesting that theoretical perspectives beyond sampling may be necessary.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work on large language models (LLMs) has demonstrated the value of <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inference-time interventions</em> that <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">steer</em> models towards higher-quality outputs. For instance, existing work has experimented with a combination of using <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel</em> generations from LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, techniques to <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aggregate</em> such generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and ways to <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prune or compress</em> them to fit in the context window for subsequent model calls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Even with <em id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no additional training</em>, such interventions can significantly improve performance on challenging tasks such as mathematical reasoning and question answering. However, these methods are largely ad hoc, since we lack a unifying theoretical framework that can <em id="S1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explain</em> the benefits of different inference-time interventions and <em id="S1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">guide</em> algorithm design towards the most effective ones.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07887v1/figures/math500-smc-vs-bon/scatter-diff-n-32-vs-bon-n-32.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/140;" width="143" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of SMC (with <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles) vs. Best-of-<math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> on Math500 problems; here we take <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=32</annotation></semantics></math>. Each point is a different problem; thus, SMC with <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles improves performance over Best-of-<math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> on most problems. See <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 SMC on Math Problem-solving Tasks ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> for details.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we ask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Can we analyze inference-time interventions such as aggregation and pruning of parallel generations in a principled manner?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To do so, we study the paradigm of <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">guided LLM generation with a process reward model</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, where the process reward model numerically scores partial generations. In this paradigm, aggregation and pruning are naturally implemented by <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">particle filtering</em> algorithms, including the popular <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC)</em> method, which use the process reward model to adaptively prune and replicate multiple <em id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">particles</em> (namely, partial generations from the base model), as tokens are generated. These strategies can have robust empirical benefits over simpler interventions such as Best-of-<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>; see e.g. <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al. (2025b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While particle filtering methods have been extensively applied to tasks ranging from posterior inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pitt and Shephard, 1997</a>)</cite> and robot localization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Montemerlo et al., 2002</a>)</cite> to steering LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipkin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lew et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loula et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grand et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skreta et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, few non-asymptotic <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">guarantees</em> have been established for such methods. In this paper, we address this challenge, providing a user-friendly analysis of SMC and identifying benefits and limitations of more sophisticated particle filtering methods.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Towards a theory of inference-time interventions</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Increasingly, inference-time interventions utilize models not just for generation and scoring, but also more complex operations such as splicing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or summarizing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> generations. While these operations are not directly captured by our setting, we believe that our work is a necessary first step towards developing a framework for understanding (and improving) such interventions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Setting: Guided Generation with Imperfect Process Rewards</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a fixed prompt, a language model defines a distribution <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{H})</annotation></semantics></math> over sequences of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">actions</em> in some set <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            For simplicity, we assume that the language model produces generations of fixed length.</span></span></span>
One can think of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as the space of <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tokens</em>, or more generally as blocks of tokens. Given a sequence <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can efficiently sample from <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{h+1}=\cdot\mid{}a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{h+1}=\cdot\mid{}a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math>, which enables autoregressive sampling from <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We model the task of <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">steering</em> the language model towards some desired reward as a <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sampling</em> problem, in line with recent work on LLM inference and post-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geuter et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foster et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu and Lu, 2026</a>)</cite>. Given query access to a reward function <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}:\mathcal{A}^{H}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}:\mathcal{A}^{H}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we aim to sample from the <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tilted distribution</em> <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{H}(a_{1:H})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:H})r^{\star}(a_{1:H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{H}(a_{1:H})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:H})r^{\star}(a_{1:H})</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, in the context of mathematical reasoning, <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r^{\star}(a_{1:H})\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(a_{1:H})\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> measures whether <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:H}</annotation></semantics></math> correctly solves the prompt.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Imperfect process rewards</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we theoretically formalize a process reward model (PRM) as an <em id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximate value function</em>. Given a sequence <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h}</annotation></semantics></math> of any length, the PRM provides an estimate <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{V}(a_{1:h})\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}(a_{1:h})\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> of the
<em id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expected reward</em> of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{\star}(a_{1:h}):=\mathbb{E}_{a^{\prime}_{1:H}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref}}}[r^{\star}(a^{\prime}_{1:H})\mid a^{\prime}_{1:h}=a_{1:h}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}(a_{1:h}):=\mathbb{E}_{a^{\prime}_{1:H}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref}}}[r^{\star}(a^{\prime}_{1:H})\mid a^{\prime}_{1:h}=a_{1:h}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, there has been extensive effort to train transformers specifically to be PRMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for certain problems, such as math reasoning. If such a fine-tuned PRM adapted to a desired reward function <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is not available, there are several other popular choices for <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>—e.g. LLM-as-a-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stephan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or LLM log-probabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karan and Du, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lew et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; see also <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key algorithmic challenge in this setting is that the PRM is imperfect. To address this challenge, numerous parallel and sequential approaches have been proposed, including SMC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, block rejection sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mudgal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and backtracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Botta et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Thus far, provable guarantees are only known for backtracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> — an inherently sequential approach. We ask: <em id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">given access to <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> as above, what structural properties enable approximate sampling from <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">via efficient parallel methods such as particle filtering</span>?</em></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference</h3>

<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first result identifies two <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"> key properties</span> that enable the success of SMC. For <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h},\widehat{\pi}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h},\widehat{\pi}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{h})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">true</em> and <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximate intermediate target distributions</em>, defined by <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}(a_{1:h})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})V^{\star}(a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}(a_{1:h})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})V^{\star}(a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}(a_{1:h})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})\widehat{V}(a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}(a_{1:h})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})\widehat{V}(a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we show the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">[Corollary of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>]

Suppose that the following hold.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"> (1)</span> Bounded <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action-level coverage</em>: for all <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{\star}(a_{h+1}\mid a_{1:h})/\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{h+1}\mid a_{1:h})\leq C_{\sf act}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}(a_{h+1}\mid a_{1:h})/\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{h+1}\mid a_{1:h})\leq C_{\sf act}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"> (2)</span> Bounded <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences</em> (these control the error of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, per <a href="#A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" title="VGB ( , ) ‣ Appendix A Comparison with Prior Work ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>): <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)\leq C_{\chi^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)\leq C_{\chi^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then SMC with <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles samples from a distribution <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\widehat{\mu},\pi^{\star}\right)\leq\sqrt{\frac{H^{2}C_{\sf act}(C_{\chi^{2}}+1)}{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\widehat{\mu},\pi^{\star}\right)\leq\sqrt{\frac{H^{2}C_{\sf act}(C_{\chi^{2}}+1)}{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
This bound strengthens the guarantees for the backtracking-based algorithm VGB of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Moreover, the parallel runtime of SMC is <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(H)</annotation></semantics></math> whereas VGB uses time <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(H^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(H^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> (and is inherently sequential). See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Comparison with Prior Work ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for additional discussion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{remark}</span>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">[Connection to literature on refinements of SMC]
Interestingly, the two <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"> key properties</span> listed above map quite cleanly onto various efforts in prior work focused on refining SMC algorithms. The latter of the two conditions (that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> approximates <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>) is related to the line of work on <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisting targets</em> for SMC. In particular, the distributions <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced above are often referred to as (intermediate) target distributions, and prior work has explored how to choose (or learn) <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> so as to bring <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> as close as possible to <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guarniero et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heng et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lawson et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which is a natural goal in light of <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the first condition (on action-level coverage) is related to the extensive literature on changing the proposal distribution <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}</annotation></semantics></math> to better approximate the <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally optimal proposal distribution</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naesseth et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doucet et al., 2000</a>)</cite>, which is defined as the distribution <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h+1}(a_{h+1}=\cdot\mid a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h+1}(a_{h+1}=\cdot\mid a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math>. In the event that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h+1}\approx\pi^{\star}_{h+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h+1}\approx\pi^{\star}_{h+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the locally optimal proposal distribution has action-level coverage close to <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the smallest possible value. Thus, as long as <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently accurate, modifying <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}</annotation></semantics></math> so as to approximate the locally optimal proposal is a way to decrease action-level coverage.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              Indeed, using <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h+1}</annotation></semantics></math> as the proposal and appropriately modifying the weights in SMC yields a variant with improved convergence properties (see <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Faster Convergence for SMC-RS ‣ Appendix D Proofs for SMC-RS ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>).</span></span></span> Summarizing, <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">our result helps to unify the existing literature on variants of SMC by showing that they are effectively minimizing the key quantities <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution II: Beyond Sequential Monte Carlo</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can particle filtering algorithms beyond SMC achieve stronger guarantees? First, we show that if <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is close to <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> in a stronger <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> sense, then it is possible to achieve <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exponential decay</em> in sampling error (rather than polynomial decay, as in <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) by wrapping SMC in an outer rejection sampling loop (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Beyond SMC: Faster Convergence under 𝐿_∞-condition ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we observe that even when the PRM is <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">perfect</em>, and hence <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\chi^{2}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\chi^{2}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, SMC requires <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>H</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{H})</annotation></semantics></math> particles to achieve non-trivial sampling accuracy (<a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6 Lower Bound for SMC with Exact Value Function ‣ Appendix C Proofs for SMC ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>), even though there is a simple one-particle algorithm for this special case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We show that a modified algorithm— <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sequential Monte Carlo with Rejection Sampling (SMC-RS, <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)</em>—avoids this pathology, recovering <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> as well as the one-particle guarantee (<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution III: Limits of particle filtering</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key limitation of SMC (and our modifications) is that even when <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> has bounded action-level coverage (i.e. <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sf act}=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sf act}=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>) and the approximate value function <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is within a constant factor of the truth (and hence <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\chi^{2}}=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\chi^{2}}=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>), our algorithm requires maintaining <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{poly}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{poly}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> particles and hence requires superlinear work. In <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Limits of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> we show that fully avoiding this lower bound would require <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lookahead</em>: any <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">myopic</em> method needs at least <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\log H/\log\log H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\log H/\log\log H)</annotation></semantics></math> particles (<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Limits of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). Closing this gap is open.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Addendum: backtracking as a form of particle filtering</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E A Unified Perspective on Backtracking and Particle Filtering ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, we show an interesting connection between particle filtering algorithms and the backtracking-based approach VGB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> — previously, the only algorithm for inference-time steering of LLMs that was known to be robust to PRM errors. VGB is a (one-particle) Markov chain method that generates tokens but sometimes also <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deletes</em> the most recent token. We show that the execution of VGB can be <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coupled</em> with the execution of a certain particle filtering method, potentially providing a new perspective on the benefits of both methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Empirical Contributions: Does the Theory Predict the Performance of SMC in LLMs?</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate empirically whether the quantities identified by <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> indeed predict the performance of SMC on LLM sampling problems. First, we consider a simple “prompt switching” task in which the base model <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to sampling from an LLM given some prompt <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to sampling from that LLM given another prompt <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for details). By varying <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the PRM <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can control for each quantity in <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. First, in <a href="#S1.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 1.3 Empirical Contributions: Does the Theory Predict the Performance of SMC in LLMs? ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>, we consider a dataset where <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for all prompts in the dataset (so <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\chi^{2}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\chi^{2}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) but the action level coverage varies, and observe a strong correlation between the action-level coverage (measured via a KL-divergence proxy) and the sampling error of SMC. Second, in <a href="#S1.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 1.3 Empirical Contributions: Does the Theory Predict the Performance of SMC in LLMs? ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>, we consider a dataset where the action-level coverage is identical across all prompts but the degree of accuracy of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> varies, and again we observe a strong correlation between <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and the sampling error of SMC. (We report the KL divergence instead of the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> divergence since the latter requires prohibitively large sample complexity to estimate.)</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07887v1/mean-ratio-vs-logprob-diff-numparticles-32.svg" id="S1.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Action-level coverage.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07887v1/inv-phi-vs-logprob-diff-numparticles-32.svg" id="S1.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/355;" width="476" height="355"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="S1.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical validation for our theory on the <em id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt-switching task</em> (see <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). (a) We vary the action-level coverage (as measured by a KL-divergence proxy) across many prompts while keeping <math id="S1.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, and observe that action-level coverage predicts the sampling error of SMC. (b) We fix <math id="S1.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}=\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}=\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (so that action-level coverage is fixed) and observe that <math id="S1.F2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> predicts the sampling error of SMC.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluating SMC on math reasoning tasks</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we consider the performance of SMC on challenging math reasoning benchmarks including AIME and Math500. In <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we plot, for each problem in Math500, the accuracy of SMC with <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles against that of Best-of-<math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=32</annotation></semantics></math>); each of these quantities was estimated by running the requisite algorithm 100 times per problem. As most points lie below the line <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that SMC improves performance over Best-of-<math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in a <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform sense over all problems</em>, which extends prior observations in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatziveroglou (2025)</a></cite> which observed that SMC improves upon Best-of-<math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in an <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">on-average</em> sense over such benchmarks. Despite this strong evidence for superiority of SMC, as we discuss further in <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 SMC on Math Problem-solving Tasks ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, the quality of the PRM <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> (as measured by an estimate of the chi-square divergence between <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>) does not seem to positively correlate with performance, as often <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">larger divergence</em> leads to <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">higher accuracy</em>. <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">We leave as an intriguing open question the development of a more refined framework that captures performance on such benchmarks.</em></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Roadmap</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> introduces the formal setting for our theoretical results. <a href="#S3" title="3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> contains our main theoretical results, and <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> contains our experimental results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our theory applies to a setting that generalizes <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Setting: Guided Generation with Imperfect Process Rewards ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. It should capture settings such as guidance for diffusion models, though we do not investigate such settings concretely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{0},\mathcal{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{X}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{0},\mathcal{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{X}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary sets, and let <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\mathcal{X}_{0}\sqcup\mathcal{X}_{1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\mathcal{X}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\mathcal{X}_{0}\sqcup\mathcal{X}_{1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\mathcal{X}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be their disjoint union. Let <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}=(\pi_{\texttt{ref},h})_{h=0}^{H-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}=(\pi_{\texttt{ref},h})_{h=0}^{H-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of transition kernels <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}:\mathcal{X}_{h}\to\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}:\mathcal{X}_{h}\to\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality, assume that <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{0}=\{\perp\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⟂</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{0}=\{\perp\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a single element. The transition kernels induces a Markov chain <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{0}\to x_{1}\to x_{2}\cdots\to x_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\to x_{1}\to x_{2}\cdots\to x_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{h+1}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(\cdot\mid x_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h+1}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(\cdot\mid x_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq h\leq H-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq h\leq H-1</annotation></semantics></math>; we drop the subscript <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> when clear from context. We write <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:H}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:H}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a sample from the Markov chain. Let <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> be the induced marginal distribution of <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{remark}</span>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[Special case: autoregressive generation] In the specialization to LLMs discussed in <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we would have <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{h}=\mathcal{A}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{h}=\mathcal{A}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(\cdot\mid a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(\cdot\mid a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math> is the transition kernel which samples <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{h+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{h+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (given <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h}</annotation></semantics></math>) and appends it to <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As in the setup of <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, given a terminal reward function <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}:\mathcal{X}_{H}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}:\mathcal{X}_{H}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">value function</em> <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined, for <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, by
<math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{\star}(x)=\En_{x_{1:H}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref}}}[r^{\star}(x_{H})\mid x_{h}=x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}(x)=\En_{x_{1:H}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref}}}[r^{\star}(x_{H})\mid x_{h}=x]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}(x_{H})=r^{\star}(x_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}(x_{H})=r^{\star}(x_{H})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{H}\in\mathcal{X}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{H}\in\mathcal{X}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>, define the distribution <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}(x)\propto\pi_{h}(x)\cdot V^{\star}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}(x)\propto\pi_{h}(x)\cdot V^{\star}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. One may check that the distributions <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> are induced by the Markov chain with kernel</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{\star}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h}):=\frac{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})V^{\star}(x_{h+1})}{V^{\star}_{h}(x_{h})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{\star}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h}):=\frac{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})V^{\star}(x_{h+1})}{V^{\star}_{h}(x_{h})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">as <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{x_{h}}\pi^{\star}_{h}(x_{h})\pi^{\star}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})=\pi^{\star}_{h+1}(x_{h+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{x_{h}}\pi^{\star}_{h}(x_{h})\pi^{\star}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})=\pi^{\star}_{h+1}(x_{h+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          We note the following slight abuse of notation: we use <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> with no subscript to denote the transition kernel in <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Preliminaries ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> with a subscript to denote the corresponding intermediate <em id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributions</em>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Partial Reward Model</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the definition of the PRM to our more general setting in the natural way: the PRM is denoted <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and satisfies <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}(x_{H})=r^{\star}(x_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}(x_{H})=r^{\star}(x_{H})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{H}\in\mathcal{X}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{H}\in\mathcal{X}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Z}_{h}:=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\pi_{h}}[\widehat{V}(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Z}_{h}:=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\pi_{h}}[\widehat{V}(x)]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{X}_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> be defined by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}(x)=\pi_{h}(x)\cdot\widehat{V}(x)/\widehat{Z}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}(x)=\pi_{h}(x)\cdot\widehat{V}(x)/\widehat{Z}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Simple Criteria for Success of SMC</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Sequential Monte Carlo</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Transition kernel <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, value function <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter:</span> Number of particles <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            Initialize <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\nu}_{0}=\updelta_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">δ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\nu}_{0}=\updelta_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1,\cdots,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1,\cdots,H</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle 1}},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle N}}\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\widehat{\nu}_{h-1}(\cdot{})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">N</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">i.i.d.</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle 1}},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle N}}\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\widehat{\nu}_{h-1}(\cdot{})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              Generate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref},h-1}(\cdot\mid\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref},h-1}(\cdot\mid\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}})</annotation></semantics></math> independently for <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              Define weights <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}=\frac{\widehat{V}(x_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}})}{\widehat{V}(\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}=\frac{\widehat{V}(x_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}})}{\widehat{V}(\tilde{x}_{h-1}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              Define weighted empirical measure <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\nu}_{h}\vcentcolon={}\frac{1}{W_{h}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}\updelta_{x_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\nu}_{h}\vcentcolon={}\frac{1}{W_{h}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}\updelta_{x_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{h}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{h}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{h}^{{\scriptscriptstyle i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            Define <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{W}_{h}:=\prod_{i=1}^{h}\frac{W_{i}}{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mfrac><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{W}_{h}:=\prod_{i=1}^{h}\frac{W_{i}}{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output Option 1:</span> Output <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{H}\sim\widehat{\nu}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{H}\sim\widehat{\nu}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output Option 2:</span> With probability <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left\{\frac{\widehat{W}_{H}}{2\mathsf{C}_{\infty}\widehat{V}(\perp)},1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⟂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left\{\frac{\widehat{W}_{H}}{2\mathsf{C}_{\infty}\widehat{V}(\perp)},1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, output <math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\widehat{\nu}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\widehat{\nu}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and otherwise restart the algorithm.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first criterion is bounded <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action-level coverage</em>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{assumption}</span>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">There exists parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sf act}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sf act}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}_{h+1}(x_{h+1})\leq C_{\sf act}V^{\star}_{h}(x_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}_{h+1}(x_{h+1})\leq C_{\sf act}V^{\star}_{h}(x_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{h+1}\in\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot\mid x_{h}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h+1}\in\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot\mid x_{h}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We call this condition <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action-level coverage</em> because
<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{V^{\star}(x_{h+1})}{V^{\star}(x_{h})}=\frac{\pi^{\star}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})}{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{V^{\star}(x_{h+1})}{V^{\star}(x_{h})}=\frac{\pi^{\star}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})}{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(x_{h+1}\mid x_{h})}</annotation></semantics></math>
is exactly the density ratio between the conditional distributions <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We remark that <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>
can be relaxed to the <em id="footnote4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average</em> sense (cf. <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Proofs for SMC ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>).</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The second criterion is bounded <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences between <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Quantitatively, the two criteria yield the following guarantee for SMC, which directly implies <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Contribution I: Simple criteria for success of SMC ‣ 1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Under <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, SMC with <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles achieves</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\sqrt{\frac{C_{\sf act}}{N}}\left(H+\sum_{h=1}^{H-1}\sqrt{D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msqrt><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\sqrt{\frac{C_{\sf act}}{N}}\left(H+\sum_{h=1}^{H-1}\sqrt{D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that bounding <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> only requires <em id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average-case</em> closeness between <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" title="VGB ( , ) ‣ Appendix A Comparison with Prior Work ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). Existing theory for SMC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schweizer, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu and Lu, 2026</a>)</cite> can recover an analogous bound that requires <em id="S3.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">worst-case</em> closeness:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{assumption}</span>
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">There exists parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{\infty}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{\infty}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{-1}\leq\frac{V^{\star}(x)}{\widehat{V}(x)}\leq\mathsf{C}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{-1}\leq\frac{V^{\star}(x)}{\widehat{V}(x)}\leq\mathsf{C}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">While the proof technique of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schweizer (2012)</a></cite> can relax this assumption to some extent, it cannot recover <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (see <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Insufficiency of Existing Analysis ‣ Appendix C Proofs for SMC ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>). Moreover, as we show below, we can strengthen <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> further to tolerate <em id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heavy-tailed errors</em> in <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Handling Tail Behavior</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> provide an easy-to-interpret upper bound, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is sensitive to the tail behavior of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, the tail behavior of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>); in the case of heavy-tailed errors, it can blow up and render <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> vacuous.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the goal of SMC is to produce a sample from an <em id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximately</em> correct distribution, so the tail behavior should only appear as an error probability. This motivates the following notion of distributional closeness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which can be bounded in terms of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence but not vice versa—–in particular, it can be arbitrarily small even when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{definition}</span>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For parameter <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>,
we define</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)=\mathbb{P}_{x\sim\pi^{\star}_{h}}\left(\frac{\pi^{\star}_{h}(x)}{\widehat{\pi}_{h}(x)}\geq M\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≥</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)=\mathbb{P}_{x\sim\pi^{\star}_{h}}\left(\frac{\pi^{\star}_{h}(x)}{\widehat{\pi}_{h}(x)}\geq M\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Under <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, SMC with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles achieves the following for any <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq H\sqrt{\frac{MC_{\sf act}}{N}}+\sum_{h=1}^{H-1}\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>H</mi><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>Cov</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq H\sqrt{\frac{MC_{\sf act}}{N}}+\sum_{h=1}^{H-1}\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Let us compare the guarantee of <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 Handling Tail Behavior ‣ 3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a> with that of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>: the coverage terms <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 Handling Tail Behavior ‣ 3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a> play the role of the chi-square divergences <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, capturing the amount by which <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> deviates from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. A key difference, though, is that for any fixed value of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the TV error between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 Handling Tail Behavior ‣ 3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a> does not approach <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., we pay for the mass <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{M}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">additively</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Beyond SMC: Faster Convergence under <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-condition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we show that a simple modification to SMC (Output Option 2 of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) yields exponential (rather than polynomial) convergence when <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are close in a worst-case sense (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Under <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with <span id="S3.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output Option 2</span> samples from a distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\mu,\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\mu,\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
as long as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq\Omega(H\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{4}C_{\sf act}^{2}\log(\mathsf{C}_{\infty}H/\delta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq\Omega(H\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{4}C_{\sf act}^{2}\log(\mathsf{C}_{\infty}H/\delta))</annotation></semantics></math>, using a number of outer loop steps that is bounded by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{2}\log(1/\delta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{2}\log(1/\delta))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The basic idea is to wrap SMC in an outer rejection sampling loop. This qualitatively matches the convergence rate of the backtracking-based method VGB of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which requires the same assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the PRM is perfect), the upper bound in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> still scales as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{H}{\sqrt{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>H</mi><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{H}{\sqrt{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, this is a fundamental limitation of SMC, not an artifact of the analysis: even when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sf act}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sf act}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, SMC cannot achieve <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> sampling error without at least <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq\Omega(\sqrt{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>H</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq\Omega(\sqrt{H})</annotation></semantics></math> particles (<a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6 Lower Bound for SMC with Exact Value Function ‣ Appendix C Proofs for SMC ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>).<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We leave the problem of determining the optimal <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-dependence as an open question.</span></span></span> Note that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, the one-particle algorithm which simply samples <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{h}\sim\widehat{\pi}(x_{h}\mid x_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h}\sim\widehat{\pi}(x_{h}\mid x_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math> succeeds in exactly sampling from <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, so there is indeed hope to improve the upper bound of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H/\sqrt{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/\sqrt{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Sequential Monte Carlo with Rejection Sampling </figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Transition kernel <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, value function <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter:</span> initial sample size <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, parameter <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            Initialize <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="alg2.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\perp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⟂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\perp</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1,\cdots,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1,\cdots,H</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              Initialize a multi-set <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{h}=\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{h}=\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{h}|&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{h}|&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{h-1}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\mathcal{S}_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h-1}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\mathcal{S}_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{h}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref},h-1}(\cdot\mid x_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h}\sim\pi_{\texttt{ref},h-1}(\cdot\mid x_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               With probability <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\widehat{V}(x_{h})}{\eta\widehat{V}(x_{h-1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\widehat{V}(x_{h})}{\eta\widehat{V}(x_{h-1})}</annotation></semantics></math>, update <math id="alg2.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{h}\leftarrow\mathcal{S}_{h}\cup\{x_{h}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{h}\leftarrow\mathcal{S}_{h}\cup\{x_{h}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
  
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\widehat{\nu}_{H}=\mathsf{Unif}(\mathcal{S}_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\widehat{\nu}_{H}=\mathsf{Unif}(\mathcal{S}_{H})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate this issue, we introduce Sequential Monte Carlo with Rejection Sampling (SMC-RS, <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Intuitively, SMC-RS fixes the issue that, in order to sample new particles, SMC <em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalizes</em> the weighted empirical measure, which introduces interference between particles. SMC-RS instead uses rejection sampling, so that regardless of the other particles, the conditional distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{h}\mid{}x_{h-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h}\mid{}x_{h-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\widehat{\pi}(x_{h}\mid{}x_{h-1})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(x_{h}\mid{}x_{h-1})\widehat{V}(x_{h})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\widehat{\pi}(x_{h}\mid{}x_{h-1})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(x_{h}\mid{}x_{h-1})\widehat{V}(x_{h}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This can be regarded as a refinement of the base proposal <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot\mid{}x_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot\mid{}x_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math> with the knowledge of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, when <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math>, SMC-RS reduces to <em id="S3.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action-level rejection sampling</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Klein, 2021</a>)</cite>, which, due to <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Preliminaries ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, is an exact sampler when <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Comparison with Prior Work ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for the definition).
We analyze SMC-RS under the following action-level coverage condition, which we view as conceptually equivalent to <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (and formally equivalent, up to a factor of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> condition of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). In the event that we make the weaker assumption of controlling chi-square divergence between <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> (as in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> below), <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is needed in order to ensure that a bounded value of the parameter <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> suffices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{assumption}</span>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">There exists parameter <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{h+1}(x_{h+1})\leq\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}\widehat{V}_{h}(x_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{h+1}(x_{h+1})\leq\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}\widehat{V}_{h}(x_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{h+1}\in\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot\mid x_{h}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{h+1}\in\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot\mid x_{h}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Under <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, suppose that SMC-RS (<a href="#alg2" title="In 3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is instantiated with <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\geq\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\geq\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
SMC-RS with <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> particles achieves</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\sqrt{D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><msqrt><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\sqrt{D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and has time complexity <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(NH\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(NH\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> in expectation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, suppose <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is near-accurate so that <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)\leq\epsilon^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)\leq\epsilon^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then SMC-RS achieves</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\frac{\epsilon H}{\sqrt{N}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\En[\widehat{\nu}_{H}],\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq\frac{\epsilon H}{\sqrt{N}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\leq\frac{1}{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\leq\frac{1}{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, then SMC-RS achieves sampling error <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> with only <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> particles. This is in sharp contrast to SMC, where by <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6 Lower Bound for SMC with Exact Value Function ‣ Appendix C Proofs for SMC ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq\Omega(\sqrt{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>H</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq\Omega(\sqrt{H})</annotation></semantics></math> particles are necessary even when <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}=V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}=V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sf act}=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sf act}=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (note that in this setting <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}=C_{\sf act}=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{C}_{\mathsf{act}}=C_{\sf act}=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> as well, so SMC-RS does indeed succeed).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, SMC-RS admits analogues of <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 Handling Tail Behavior ‣ 3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a> (heavy-tailed errors) and <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Beyond SMC: Faster Convergence under 𝐿_∞-condition ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> (fast-rate convergence); see <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Analysis of SMC-RS with Heavy-tailed Errors ‣ Appendix D Proofs for SMC-RS ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Faster Convergence for SMC-RS ‣ Appendix D Proofs for SMC-RS ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Limits of Particle Filtering Methods</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key downside of SMC (and the discussed variants) is that, when the PRM is imperfect, avoiding error amplification requires the number of particles <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to grow at least linearly in the horizon <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section, we show that some horizon dependence is necessary for all <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">myopic</em> particle filtering methods. Informally, a myopic particle filtering method is any algorithm that maintains a set of particles <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, and does not use the PRM data from later steps <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;h</annotation></semantics></math> to determine the set <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. SMC and SMC-RS are two examples of myopic methods; see <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Proof of ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> for a formal definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that even with mild (constant-factor) imperfections in the PRM, any myopic particle filtering algorithm requires nearly <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\log H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\log H)</annotation></semantics></math> particles to even obtain non-trivial <em id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coverage</em> of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(H):=\log(H)/(4\log\log(H))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(H):=\log(H)/(4\log\log(H))</annotation></semantics></math>. There is <span id="S3.SS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">no myopic particle filtering algorithm</span> <span id="S3.SS4.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Alg</span> with the following guarantee for all sufficiently large <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>: <span id="S3.SS4.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Alg</span> uses at most <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(H)</annotation></semantics></math> particles, and under <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sf act}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖺𝖼𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sf act}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{\infty}=e^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{\infty}=e^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the output distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S3.SS4.p3.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Alg</span> satisfies <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nu(\mathcal{E})\geq H^{-1/5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(\mathcal{E})\geq H^{-1/5}</annotation></semantics></math>
for all events <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\subset\mathcal{X}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\subset\mathcal{X}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{H}(\mathcal{E})\geq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{H}(\mathcal{E})\geq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As an immediate corollary, no such algorithm can achieve <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\nu,\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq 1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{TV}}\left(\nu,\pi^{\star}_{H}\right)\leq 1/3</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, with a perfect PRM (i.e. <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{\infty}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{\infty}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), a single particle achieves zero error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohatgi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Moreover, <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Limits of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is particularly striking since, under <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, every intermediate distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> has constant coverage of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}(x)/\pi^{\star}_{h}(x)\geq\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}(x)/\pi^{\star}_{h}(x)\geq\mathsf{C}_{\infty}^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>). <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Limits of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> shows that it is impossible for myopic methods to inductively “maintain” this coverage without a super-constant number of particles — and raises the question of whether some form of <em id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lookahead</em> can improve computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments in Language Models</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we investigate to what extent the quantities from <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> predict the performance of SMC on sampling problems involving LLMs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Controlled Setting: Prompt-Switching Task</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key challenge in evaluating SMC and understanding its performance as a function of the aforementioned quantities is the difficulty of <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">measuring them</em>, as well as measuring the performance of SMC itself. Most notably, we need to measure quantities involving the target distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is typically not readily available. While prior work (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2024)</a></cite>) has studied how to evaluate the performance of SMC using the algorithm’s estimate of the partition function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{ref}}}[V^{\star}(x_{H})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\texttt{ref}}}[V^{\star}(x_{H})]</annotation></semantics></math>, such techniques are only guaranteed to provide tight bounds if SMC is accurate. Moreover, these methods do not directly address the challenge of estimating other quantities such as action-level coverage.
Thus, in this section, we consider an alternative approach, in a setting which will conveniently satisfy that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}},\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}},\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are both efficiently sampleable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we consider the <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt-switching task</em>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the output distribution of a language model under different prompts. Formally, we fix a language model <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the setting of <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the token space of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{h}=\mathcal{A}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{h}=\mathcal{A}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each instance of the prompt-switching task is determined by 3 prompts, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star},\texttt{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star},\texttt{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows.
Given choices of these prompts, we let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the output distribution of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> for the prompt <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we denote it as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot)=\mathsf{M}(\cdot\mid\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(\cdot)=\mathsf{M}(\cdot\mid\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We then choose the terminal reward function to be <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r^{\star}(a_{1:H}):=\frac{\mathsf{M}(a_{1:H}\mid\texttt{p}^{\star})}{\mathsf{M}(a_{1:H}\mid\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(a_{1:H}):=\frac{\mathsf{M}(a_{1:H}\mid\texttt{p}^{\star})}{\mathsf{M}(a_{1:H}\mid\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives that, for any intermediate step <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{\star}(a_{1:h})={\mathsf{M}(a_{1:h}\mid\texttt{p}^{\star})/\mathsf{M}(a_{1:h}\mid\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}(a_{1:h})={\mathsf{M}(a_{1:h}\mid\texttt{p}^{\star})/\mathsf{M}(a_{1:h}\mid\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}(a_{1:h})=\mathsf{M}(a_{1:h}\mid\texttt{p}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}(a_{1:h})=\mathsf{M}(a_{1:h}\mid\texttt{p}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since it is tractable to sample from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and compute sequence-level probabilities, sampling from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}},\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}},\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as computing <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is tractable. Finally, the prompt <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">p</span> determines <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, in a way that will be discussed below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate SMC on the prompt-switching task, we consider the following metric, inspired by more complex “LLM-as-judge” techniques and also the use of log-probabilities to characterize the output distributions of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Golowich et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Given two distributions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu,\nu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu,\nu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over sequences of length <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and a set of prompts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-logprob discrepancy</em> <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\nu,\nu^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>LPD</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\nu,\nu^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as the difference between the mean token-level log-probabilities assigned to sequences sampled from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the prompts in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> (summed over all positions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>). Formally, for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu,\nu^{\prime}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu,\nu^{\prime}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\nu,\nu^{\prime}):=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>LPD</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\nu,\nu^{\prime}):=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{\texttt{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\left|\mathbb{E}_{a_{1:H}\sim\nu}\left[\log\mathsf{M}(a_{h}\mid\texttt{p},a_{1:h-1})\right]\right.\left.-\mathbb{E}_{a_{1:H}^{\prime}\sim\nu^{\prime}}\left[\log\mathsf{M}(a_{h}^{\prime}\mid\texttt{p},a_{1:h-1}^{\prime})\right]\right|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{\texttt{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\left|\mathbb{E}_{a_{1:H}\sim\nu}\left[\log\mathsf{M}(a_{h}\mid\texttt{p},a_{1:h-1})\right]\right.\left.-\mathbb{E}_{a_{1:H}^{\prime}\sim\nu^{\prime}}\left[\log\mathsf{M}(a_{h}^{\prime}\mid\texttt{p},a_{1:h-1}^{\prime})\right]\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In words, the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-logprob discrepancy uses the prompts in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> as <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test functions</em> to measure how different the two distributions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu,\nu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu,\nu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are. It can be thought of as a very simple (albeit more stable) “LLM-as-judge” metric, where instead of <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generating</em> from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu,\nu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu,\nu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply use the log-probabilities assigned by the model <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our evaluations, we choose <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is natural in light of the fact that the two most salient prompts in the prompt switching task are <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Evaluating Dependence on PRM Accuracy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the influence of PRM accuracy on SMC performance, we fix the action-level coverage between <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (in particular, by fixing <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}=\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}=\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>) and vary the PRM <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we consider a number <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=50</annotation></semantics></math> of instances of the prompt-switching task as described above: each instance <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> is specified by three prompts <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})^{(i)},(\texttt{p}^{\star})^{(i)},\texttt{p}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})^{(i)},(\texttt{p}^{\star})^{(i)},\texttt{p}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as a scalar <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> used in <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2 Evaluating Dependence on PRM Accuracy ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> below. In this subsection, the reference <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}})^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\texttt{p}^{\star})^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\texttt{p}^{\star})^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> prompts are shared across all instances, so we denote them by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Moreover, we in fact have <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}=\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}=\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> --- this prompt asks the model to write a short story (e.g., ‘‘Write a scene about a dragon negotiating peace with the last human kingdom.’’).<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            While in this setting it is trivial to sample from <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, we emphasize that our main goal here is studying the performance of SMC in terms of PRM accuracy as opposed to solving a challenging sampling problem.</span></span></span> Each instance <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> has a different value of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is given as follows: we fix <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> prompts <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{(1)},\ldots,\texttt{p}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{(1)},\ldots,\texttt{p}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are each identical to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> except they ask for the story in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> different styles (e.g., “<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> Tell it as a news article.”). For each such prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{(i)}(a_{1:H})=\mathsf{M}(a_{1:H}\mid\texttt{p}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(i)}(a_{1:H})=\mathsf{M}(a_{1:H}\mid\texttt{p}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, which should be interpreted as a “perturbed” version of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we define</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h}):=\frac{\pi^{\star}(a_{1:h})}{\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})}\cdot\left(\frac{\pi^{(i)}(a_{1:h})}{\pi^{\star}(a_{1:h})}\right)^{(1-h/H)\cdot\alpha}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h}):=\frac{\pi^{\star}(a_{1:h})}{\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})}\cdot\left(\frac{\pi^{(i)}(a_{1:h})}{\pi^{\star}(a_{1:h})}\right)^{(1-h/H)\cdot\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for some parameter <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controlling the degree of accuracy of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. To get some intuition behind this definition, suppose <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity: for small <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h})\approx{\pi^{(i)}(a_{1:h})}/{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(a_{1:h})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h})\approx{\pi^{(i)}(a_{1:h})}/{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(a_{1:h})}</annotation></semantics></math>, while at the final step <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:H})=V^{\star}(a_{1:H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:H})=V^{\star}(a_{1:H})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, at early steps, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{h}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{h}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> looks the value function of the “perturbed” target distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that for small <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{h}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{h}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is “misleading” in that it “points towards” <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. For larger <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, we attenuate the noise so as to ensure that sampling from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is information-theoretically possible (i.e., that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{H}^{(i)}=V^{\star}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{H}^{(i)}=V^{\star}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Natural variation in the choice of the prompts <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> (which were generated by GPT-5) leads the resulting functions <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to have varying levels of accuracy with respect to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{h})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}(a_{1:h})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})\cdot\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}(a_{1:h})\propto\pi_{\texttt{ref}}(a_{1:h})\cdot\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math>. While estimating <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is in principle possible, we found that the ratios <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{\star}(a_{1:h})/\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}(a_{1:h})/\widehat{V}^{(i)}(a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math> needed to estimate the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> divergence are prohibitively high in variance.
Thus, we instead estimate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}^{(i)}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to measure the divergence between <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is much more stable because KL divergence can be written as an expectation of <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log</em>-probabilities (see <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Details for ‣ Appendix G Additional Experimental Details ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a> for further details).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we use Qwen3-0.6B as the language model <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and set the horizon to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=64</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. For each choice of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> we run SMC with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=32</annotation></semantics></math> particles given the PRM <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and sample from its output distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\nu}_{H}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\nu}_{H}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#S1.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 1.3 Empirical Contributions: Does the Theory Predict the Performance of SMC in LLMs? ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>, we plot the values of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}^{(i)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\,\|\,\widehat{\pi}_{h}^{(i)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> against the sampling error of SMC, measured via <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-logprob discrepancies <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathbb{E}[\widehat{\nu}_{H}^{(i)}],\pi^{\star}_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>LPD</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathbb{E}[\widehat{\nu}_{H}^{(i)}],\pi^{\star}_{H})</annotation></semantics></math> (estimated by running SMC for 200 trials). We observe a clear correlation between the two quantities, thus validating our theoretical findings that PRM accuracy (as measured by the divergence between <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>) predicts SMC’s sampling error.
In <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Details for ‣ Appendix G Additional Experimental Details ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a> we repeat the experiment for additional choices of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star},\texttt{p}^{(i)},\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{\star},\texttt{p}^{(i)},\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and observe similar results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Evaluating Dependence on Action-level Coverage</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluate the influence of action-level coverage on SMC performance. We consider the same setup as in the previous subsection, except for each instance <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> we take the target distribution, denoted <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, to be the same as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, each instance is determined by the tuple <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{(i)},\texttt{p}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}},\texttt{p}^{(i)},\texttt{p}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we take <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}=(V^{\star})^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}=(V^{\star})^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this setting, the <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action-level coverage</em> depends on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. We again use Qwen3-0.6B, take the horizon to be <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=32</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, and run SMC with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=32</annotation></semantics></math> particles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the action-level coverage between <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> (in the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> sense of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) is extremely large and essentially impossible to compute, we note that <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left((\pi^{\star})^{(i)}\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left((\pi^{\star})^{(i)}\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as an “average-case proxy” for action-level coverage in light of the chain rule, which gives</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left((\pi^{\star})^{(i)}\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\right)=\sum_{h=1}^{H}\mathbb{E}_{a_{1:h}\sim(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}_{h}}\left[\log\frac{(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}_{h}(a_{h}\mid a_{1:h-1})}{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(a_{h}\mid a_{1:h-1})}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left((\pi^{\star})^{(i)}\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\right)=\sum_{h=1}^{H}\mathbb{E}_{a_{1:h}\sim(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}_{h}}\left[\log\frac{(\pi^{\star})^{(i)}_{h}(a_{h}\mid a_{1:h-1})}{\pi_{\texttt{ref},h}(a_{h}\mid a_{1:h-1})}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 1.3 Empirical Contributions: Does the Theory Predict the Performance of SMC in LLMs? ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> plots <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{1}{H}D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left((\pi^{\star})^{(i)}\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖪𝖫</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{H}D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left((\pi^{\star})^{(i)}\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{ref}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> against the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-logprob discrepancies <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathbb{E}[\widehat{\nu}_{H}^{(i)}],\pi^{\star}_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>LPD</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LPD}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathbb{E}[\widehat{\nu}_{H}^{(i)}],\pi^{\star}_{H})</annotation></semantics></math>; we again observe a clear correlation between the two quantities, in line with our theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Dependence on the Number of Particles</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.4 Dependence on the Number of Particles ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we show the performance of SMC as a function of the number of particles <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for the prompt switching task, using a setup similar to that of <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Evaluating Dependence on PRM Accuracy ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. In particular, we consider 50 different prompt tuples <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}^{(i)},(\texttt{p}^{\star})^{(i)},\texttt{p}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\texttt{p}_{\texttt{ref}}^{(i)},(\texttt{p}^{\star})^{(i)},\texttt{p}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each tuple we use a PRM <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> defined from <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{p}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">p</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{p}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> as in <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2 Evaluating Dependence on PRM Accuracy ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition to SMC, we also plot the performance of (a) sequential importance sampling using <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Comparison with Prior Work ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), and (b) the “best-of-<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>” algorithm which samples <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sequences from <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs the one with the highest value according to the terminal reward <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(V^{\star})^{(i)}(a_{1:H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V^{\star})^{(i)}(a_{1:H})</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that SMC consistently outperforms both baselines, and moreover that its performance improves as <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases; the latter conclusion is in line with our theoretical results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07887v1/wallclock-scaling-plot.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/169;" width="286" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of SMC on the prompt-switching task (<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Dependence on the Number of Particles ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). Each point represents SMC with some number of particles <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{2,4,8,\ldots,128\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{2,4,8,\ldots,128\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We average the sampling error and wall-clock time over all data points and all trials per data point. SMC consistently outperforms both sequential importance sampling (SIS) and Best-of-<math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN) baselines.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>SMC on Math Problem-solving Tasks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we study the use of SMC to improve the performance of LLMs on math problem-solving tasks, including AIME and MATH500. In this setting, the base distribution <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the output distribution of a language model prompted to solve a math problem, and the terminal reward function <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r^{\star}(a_{1:H})\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(a_{1:H})\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator that the answer <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:H}</annotation></semantics></math> is correct. Thus, <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the base model distribution <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conditioned on producing a correct answer</em>, and <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{\star}(a_{1:h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}(a_{1:h})</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability that <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a correct answer conditioned on the prefix <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are several open-source process reward models fine-tuned for math problems, which purport to yield good estimates of <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our experiments, we take the PRM <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to be Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B, and the base model <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be Qwen2.5-1B-Instruct. In the application of SMC to math reasoning tasks, since the goal is simply to find <em id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">some</em> correct answer, it is typical to combine SMC with Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampling in the following manner: at the end of SMC, we select the single particle with the highest value <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{V}(a_{1:H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}(a_{1:H})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this is distinct from the standard Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampling procedure, in which <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples are drawn <em id="S4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independently</em> from <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the one with highest <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-value is selected.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SMC improves performance on math</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many papers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puri et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatziveroglou, 2025</a>)</cite> have reported that SMC typically improves the performance over Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (as well as other baselines). However, such results are typically reported <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">on-average</em> over all problems in the dataset, which fails to account for the hetereogeneity between individual problems. For instance, it was not clear if SMC improves performance over Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> on <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> problems, or if SMC can actually hurt performance (compared to Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>) on a non-negligible fraction of the dataset.<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              Indeed, much prior work has highlighted that SMC can suffer from pathologies such as mode collapse, which might decrease the diversity of responses from which Best-of-<math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> can choose.</span></span></span> Certainly such a distinction is salient for any theoretical explanation for SMC’s performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we estimate the performance of SMC and Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for each problem in Math500 (using 100 trials per problem), and plot the accuracy of SMC against that of Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. While there is variability in the relative performance of the two algorithms, we see that for the vast majority of problems, SMC’s accuracy is at least as large as that of Best-of-<math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#A7.F5" title="In G.4 SMC vs. Best-of-𝑁 on AIME ‣ Appendix G Additional Experimental Details ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we observe a similar conclusion for AIME24 and AIME25.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07887v1/scatter-diff-intermed-256.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/184;" width="429" height="184" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Influence of PRM error (measured by <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>) on SMC accuracy for 10 Math500 problems. Different colors correspond to different values of the inverse temperature parameter <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The plots show SMC accuracy with respect to a <em id="S4.F4.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">random</em> particle (not the best one) selected at the end of SMC.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SMC and PRM accuracy</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having established that SMC leads to a consistent increase in accuracy across most Math500 and AIME problems, we next turn to the question of what factors influence its performance <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">on a given problem</em>. In light of the fact that significant effort has been spent designing better PRMs <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, a natural follow-up question from our theory is whether the divergence between <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (as measured by, e.g., the <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>) predicts the performance of SMC.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this question, we consider a range of PRMs <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math> by varying the inverse temperature parameter <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> used by the PRM to produce its final probabilities. In <a href="#S4.F4" title="In SMC improves performance on math ‣ 4.5 SMC on Math Problem-solving Tasks ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we select 10 Math500 problems, and for each value of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we plot an empirical estimate of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> against the accuracy of SMC given <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}^{(\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}^{(\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Interestingly, we observe that larger <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence often leads to <em id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">higher</em> accuracy, which is in contrast to our theoretical findings which predict the opposite. We suspect that larger values of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (lower temperature) lead to better performance since they more effectively weed out incorrect partial solutions; however, they will also potentially lead <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to miss some modes of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, which causes the <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence to blow up. Thus, in order to accurately capture the performance of SMC on such problems, we need a modification to our framework that aims to not approximate <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in a distributional sense but rather to simply cover some portion of its mass.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we have established theoretical guarantees for particle filtering algorithms under varioues assumptions relating the reference distribution <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\texttt{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\texttt{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target distribution <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and the PRM <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> (via the induced distributions <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>). We highlight below some intriguing directions for future work:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are various polynomial gaps between our upper and lower bounds, including their the dependence on horizon <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, as discussed in <a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2 Theoretical Contributions: A Principled Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods for LLM Inference ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, as well as the decay of the total variation distance as a function of <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>: whereas our upper bounds of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Simple Criteria for Success of SMC ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Beyond SMC: Near-perfect PRM ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis of Particle Filtering Methods ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> decay as <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the lower bound of <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6 Lower Bound for SMC with Exact Value Function ‣ Appendix C Proofs for SMC ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a> decays as only <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can we develop metrics for the performance of SMC (and other inference-time sampling algorithms) which are weaker than total variation distance but still meaningfully capture “usefulness” of outputs? For instance, for math problems, typically one only desires a correct answer, and the particular phrasing of the solution may not matter too much.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can we develop provable guarantees for particle filtering algorithms or other types of inference-time sampling procedures which rely on weaker notions of discrepancy between <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\star}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\star}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>? The values of <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}\left(\pi^{\star}_{h}\;\|\;{}\widehat{\pi}_{h}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> seen in, e.g., the experiments in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Evaluating Dependence on PRM Accuracy ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Evaluating Dependence on Action-level Coverage ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Dependence on the Number of Particles ‣ 4 Experiments in Language Models ‣ Reject, Resample, Repeat: Understanding Parallel Reasoning in Language Model Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> are extremely large (in particular, much larger than the values of <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> seen in our experiments), yet particle filtering algorithms still are able to effectively make use of <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
